#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "3638fd11-5f61-5656-89c4-18ee0a4318ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-21720" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t = t_0 = 0$, we have\n$e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(0)|= e^{At}x(0); = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{3(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we have\n$e^{A(t - s)}g(s)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{3(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3(t - s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThus, we obtain\n$∫_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds|= ∫_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}ds; = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}e^{-3s + 3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_{s = 0}^{s = t}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}e^{3t} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/id #uuid "c24150ed-748b-5069-9fc5-48d4e6deaef2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21721" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L2}$ and $\\textrm{L3}$. Starting from\n$\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$\nin $\\textrm{L2}$ and substituting $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$ from the original system gives\n$y^{′}(t)|=-Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}(Ax(t) + g(t)); =-Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + Ae^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t) + e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t); = e^{-A(t - t_{0})}g(t)$.\nThus, $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Evaluating $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$ at $t=t_0$ gives\n$\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=I\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $t = t_{0}$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L8}$. Multiplying both sides of\n$\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$\nfrom $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t-t_0)}$ gives\n$e^{A(t - t_{0})}y(t)|= e^{A(t - t_{0})}e^{-A(t - t_{0})}x(t); = x(t)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t - t_{0})}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/id #uuid "e151cd67-cf96-5378-9380-e9960c1eb6b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21722" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nFill in the blanks in the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} & te^{4t} \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$*\n\n$x(t)|= \\begin{bmatrix}X X X X \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix} + ∫_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ X X\\end{bmatrix}ds$." :question/worked-solution "Given the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} & te^{4t} \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ So,\n$e^{A(t - 1)}x(1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - 1)} & (t - 1)e^{4(t - 1)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3(t - 1)e^{4(t - 1)} \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{4(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we have\n$e^{A(t - s)}g(s)|= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4(t - s)} & (t - s)e^{4(t - s)} \\\\ 0 & e^{4(t - s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 (t - 1) e 4 (t - 1) \\\\ 3e^{4(t - 1)}\\end{bmatrix} + ∫_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}ds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "8797cf8a-16d2-50d9-ae11-06f25b328ee8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228135}] :question/id #uuid "6f94a953-d6ae-5d71-b1d5-f509cb5bc306" :question/math-academy-id "q-334531" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 1,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) & \\sin (2t) \\\\ -\\sin (2t) & \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $3\\sin (2t)$ |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7ffdc9-9f9a-5626-ae51-7fda526b5946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228137} {:answer/id #uuid "9b60bd2e-e9f1-535f-b8c0-c0049ffbbcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228138} {:answer/id #uuid "16710e53-2928-58a4-94a8-8448a9d69cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228139} {:answer/id #uuid "37d5e975-eeee-5d0c-afab-73f4aa2b2413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228140}] :answer-field/id #uuid "736163e7-5385-5bb0-8576-f6f56e55ea29" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2a0ab4-ccc5-5fdb-9e01-e60199005b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228142} {:answer/id #uuid "b38ef677-b641-5040-accc-571815bc6c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228143} {:answer/id #uuid "50b5a1a2-03f9-598b-ac48-ff8c671db004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228144} {:answer/id #uuid "54620513-6509-54fa-a510-d4cf8a794fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228145}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dfaa1742-379c-5afa-977d-7f82f4923ba5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6307c39-4e1f-516e-9023-c6d5d1796f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228147} {:answer/id #uuid "f3573457-7c1e-53b3-be5e-0b43119aa995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228148} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5ca747-1823-5c58-b9db-a62c2d73c3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228149}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa48b6c7-3bdf-5b7a-be50-c80204cd586a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0be037-1284-56f4-8039-381982c5fc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the sum rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228151} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad1ec6d-3a6c-5ca2-826b-f6ad4ed65aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by $e^{At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228152} {:answer/id #uuid "57d07bb3-de1b-5893-883d-d48835635778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228153} {:answer/id #uuid "62e3a038-a1c0-5359-b4f7-a6e7b0f4fc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $e^{At}x(0)$ to both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228154}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b897e708-3b14-534f-a6c6-daaa9bc2cad4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228150}] :question/id #uuid "3d5aa81c-82a3-50e6-9a6f-bc0b6662f32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334533" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $y(t) = e^{-At}x(t)$\n> $L2$: $y^{′}(t) =-Ae^{-At}x(t) + e^{-At}x^{′}(t)$\n> $L3$: $y^{′}(t) = e^{-At}g(t)$\n> $L4$: $y(t) - y(0) = ∫_{0}^{t}e^{-As}g(s)ds$\n> $L5$: $y(0) = x(0)$\n> $L6$: $y(t) = x(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}e^{-As}g(s)ds$\n> $L7$: $e^{At}y(t) = e^{At}x(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds$\n> $L8$: $e^{At}y(t) = x(t)$\n> $L9$: $x(t) = e^{At}x(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to\n{{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L7$ follows from line $L6$ by\n{{field-4}}."}]}
